In a LaTeX document, generating a table of contents (ToC) automatically is straightforward and involves using the `\tableofcontents` command. Here's a simple example:

```latex
\documentclass{book} % or article, report
\begin{document}

\tableofcontents % This command generates the ToC automatically

\chapter{First Chapter}
\section{First Section}
\subsection{First Subsection}

\chapter{Second Chapter}
\section{Second Section}

\end{document}
```

When you compile this LaTeX document, it will automatically generate a table of contents at the beginning, listing all chapters and sections/subsections. The `book` document class is used here, which is suitable for longer documents and includes chapter headings by default. The `\tableofcontents` command is placed after the `\begin{document}` command but before any content (chapters, sections, etc.) is declared.

This example demonstrates the basic setup for an automatically generated table of contents in a LaTeX document. Remember, the ToC will automatically update itself whenever you add, remove, or rearrange sections or chapters in your document.

